ately but require costly per-domain action-labeled data and produce low-fidelity rollouts.\n  * Pretrained video diffusion models (DynamiCrafter, Sora, Veo) generate high-fidelity video at internet scale but are non-interactive — they denoise full sequences with bidirectional temporal context, have no frame-level action conditioning, and cannot do autoregressive rollouts.\n\nThe paper argues the right move is \"model-level transfer\" of internet-scale video priors into a world model, rather than pretraining yet another world model on cross-domain action-labeled data (which is still data-hungry and yields low-fidelity output). ## Ablation Studies\n\n### Weight transfer × action guidance (Table 2; 30k-step training budget)\n\nWT variant  | AG  | FVD ↓  | FID ↓  | SSIM ↑  | LPIPS ↓  | PSNR ↑\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nShift  | —  | 29.9  | 7.85  | 0.799  | 0.185  | 21.5\nMasked  | —  | 29.4  | 7.07  | 0.824  | 0.169  | 22.9\nExtrapolative  | —  | 28.6  | 7.52  | 0.832  | 0.162  | 23.4\nMasked  | ✓  | 25.8  | 6.84  | 0.840  | 0.159  | 23.9\nExtrapolative  | ✓  | 22.4  | 6.16  | 0.839  | 0.159  | 23.9\n\nBoth causalization (Masked > Shift, Extrapolative ≈ Masked but slightly better) and action guidance (✓ rows beat — rows by 4–6 FVD) contribute independently.\n\n### Guidance scale λ sweep on CS:GO (Figure 8)\n\nPSNR / SSIM / LPIPS / DreamSim plotted as a function of λ ∈ [1.0, 4.0]. Performance improves with λ initially (alignment to action helps) but degrades at high λ due to over-sharpening. Sweet spot is around λ ≈ 2.\n\n## Limitations (as stated by authors, Sec. 6)\n\n  1. Backbone scale. DynamiCrafter at 1.1B is \"relatively lightweight.\" Authors expect larger video-diffusion backbones (NVIDIA Cosmos, Wan, etc.) to deliver better world-model fidelity but did not test due to compute.\n  2. Training time. Causalization + action conditioning take 100k gradient steps × ~7 days × 4 A100. Authors hope future methods can do this in fewer steps.\n  3. No explicit policy learning. Vid2World demonstrates Real2Sim evaluation, but the paper does not yet show that policies learned inside Vid2World transfer back to the real world (RL or model-based planning is left as future work).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWomap: AI-powered map for women's safer night journeys. | Devpost (https://devpost.com/software/womap)\nciteturn49search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.9 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # Womap: AI-powered map for women's safer night journeys. ...   * github.com\n\n## Accomplishments that we're proud of\n\nWe are proud to have developed an app that meets a real-world need, providing women with a tool for safer night travel. By integrating the \"Ask for Angela\" support platform, we offer real-time, on-the-ground assistance for women. We’re also pleased to have incorporated Gemini AI to provide even more precise and accurate safe route recommendations.\n\n## What we learned\n\nBuilding Womap, we learned the importance of user privacy, how to integrate AI effectively in a location-based service, and the value of community partnerships in enhancing safety features. This experience deepened our understanding of both technical development and social impact.\n\n## What's next for Womap:\n\n  * Expand Womap’s Safe Zone Network: We aim to partner with more local businesses to increase safe zones across cities, providing users with a wider network of trusted locations.\n  * Enhance AI and Accessibility: We plan to refine our AI algorithm for even more accurate safety assessments and introduce multilingual support to make Womap accessible to a global audience.\n\n## Built With\n\n  * css\n  * gemini\n  * google-maps\n  * html\n  * javascript\n  * openstreetmap\n\n## Try it out\n\n  * github.com\n\n#### Submitted to\n\n  * Google's Women Techmakers presents She Builds AI\n\n#### Created by\n\n  * Yixuan LONG\n\n  * Xintong Lu\n\n  * summer wang\n\n  *   *   * ## Updates\n\n